# /commit — one look, then ship it

The message is drafted on a small model at low effort: a commit message is a summary of a diff, not
a design decision, and paying the session model for it is waste. What keeps it honest is that you
see the whole plan once before anything runs.

`CONTEXT` means `node "$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/ship-coach/scripts/context.js"` — the path arrives pre-resolved.

## 1. Read the state — one call

`CONTEXT` (add `--issue N` when the user passed it). It prints the repository, branch, default branch, upstream,
remote, anything in progress, the issue guessed from the branch name, what will be staged, two
scratch file paths, the last 15 commit subjects, the stat, and the diff capped at 12 KB.

Stop before drafting when:
- **nothing to commit** — say so; done.
- **in progress** is not `(nothing)` — a merge or rebase is open; say which and stop.
- **detached HEAD** — ask for a branch name to create; it becomes the plan's branch.

## 2. Draft

- **Subject**: the style of the recent subjects, not a generic one. A `type(scope):` prefix only if
  the log uses them. Imperative, under 72 characters, says what changed.
- **Body**: why, in a few lines — the reason a reviewer needs, not a file list. Wrap at 72.
  If the diff was truncated, draft from the stat and what you saw; do not invent the rest.
- **Issue**: when there is one, the body's last line is `Fixes #N`.
- **Branch**, only when one is needed: `<type>/<issue>-<slug>` or `<type>/<slug>`, lowercase,
  hyphens, under 50 characters. Needed when on the default branch, when `--branch` was passed
  (use its name if it has one), or for a detached HEAD.

Defaults: push and PR **only** with `--pr`. Staging follows the `staging` line: what is already
staged, or everything.

## 3. Confirm once

**RISKY FILES** in the output (`.env`, keys, certificates) → ask about those first: leave them out,
or include them. Never stage one silently.

Then one a direct question to the user showing the full plan in the question text:

```
message : <subject>
          <body>
branch  : <current | new name, from where>
files   : <count, and the list if under 10>
push    : yes | no
PR      : yes -> base <default branch> | no
issue   : #N (the PR body says Fixes #N) | none
```

Options: **Run all** · **Edit message** · **Commit only** (no push, no PR) · **Cancel**.
Edit message: take the user's text (the Other field) as the new message, then show the plan again.
**No question tool** (a headless run): print the plan and stop. Nothing is staged, committed or
pushed without that one look.

## 4. Run

Write the message to the `message file` path with the Write tool; the PR body (the message body,
plus `Fixes #N`) to the `PR body file`. A file, not a quoted `-m`, so no shell can mangle it.

Every command runs in the `repository` the context printed — the session's working directory. Never
`cd` anywhere else (this plugin's own install path is not the user's repository), and run each
command on its own, not chained with `&&`, so a failed step stops the run where it failed.

```
git switch -c <branch>                    # only for a new branch
git add -A   |   git add -A -- . ':(exclude)<risky path>'   |   (nothing — already staged)
git commit -F "<message file>"
git push -u origin <branch>               # only when pushing
gh pr create --base <default> --title "<subject>" --body-file "<PR body file>"
```

Report the short hash, the branch, and the PR URL.

**Never**: `--force` / `--force-with-lease`, `--amend` on a pushed commit, `--no-verify`, a push to
the default branch. A failing pre-commit hook is the answer, not an obstacle — show its output and
stop.

| When | Do |
|---|---|
| no `origin`, gh missing or not logged in | commit locally, then print the exact push and `gh pr create` lines skipped |
| `gh pr create` says a PR exists | `gh pr view --json url -q .url` and report that URL |
| push rejected (behind remote) | stop; say so — rebasing is the user's call |

## The board

Nothing to do here. `Fixes #N` links the PR to the issue and closes it on merge; a GitHub project
moves the issue's status with its own built-in "Pull request linked to issue" workflow, switched on
once in the project's Workflows settings.

